---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { connectors, doc, piDoc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['builtin', '1. MCP is built into pi'],
  ['connect', '2. Connect a service'],
  ['verify', '3. Check and use it'],
  ['catalog', 'Connector requirements'],
  ['custom', 'Custom MCP servers'],
  ['permissions', 'Permissions and troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Connect your services"
  description="Let the agent use the services you pick. Add a service, sign in through your browser, and check the connection before you rely on it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="builtin">
    <h2>1. MCP is built into pi</h2>
    <p>
      MCP is the protocol. A connector is one configured MCP server. pi 1.2.0 and later connect MCP
      servers themselves, so there is nothing to install: open <strong
        >Settings → Extensions → MCP Connectors</strong
      > and add a service.
    </p>
    <p>
      Used the MCP adapter package before? The tab first shows a card that moves your servers to
      pi’s own <code>mcp.json</code>, backs up every file it changes, and removes the adapter. It
      lists every change before you choose <strong>Move to pi’s MCP</strong>. Sign in again to each
      service afterwards: the adapter’s tokens don’t carry over.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="connect">
    <h2>2. Add and authorize a service</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>MCP Connectors</strong>.</li><li>
        Find the service under <strong>Add a connector</strong> and fill in any required fields, like
        the Datadog site or Slack client ID.
      </li><li>
        Choose <strong>Add</strong>. Phosphor writes the configuration and starts pi’s sign-in.
      </li><li>
        pi opens your browser. Finish signing in there, and check the scopes and which account or
        workspace you’re granting.
      </li><li>
        Go back to Phosphor. The row shows the sign-in link with <strong>Open again</strong> in case you
        closed the tab, and <strong>Cancel</strong>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-connectors')}
      alt="MCP Connectors showing connected servers with their status, a Check again button, and a catalog of services in an example configuration."
      caption="A connection has a status, a scope, and a Check again button"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <p>The full-size image only has example config too. pi keeps the OAuth tokens, not Phosphor.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="verify">
    <h2>3. Check the connection, then the task</h2>
    <p>
      Opening the tab checks every enabled server once, and <strong>Check again</strong> repeats it. Each
      row then reads Up with a tool count, Needs sign-in, Down, Disconnected, or Disabled.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signing in and checking don’t call a model and need no open session, so they cost no tokens. A
      running chat picks up a new sign-in on its next message.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ask for something small and read-only, like “Find the Linear issue I specify and summarize its
      acceptance criteria. Do not change it.” Check the tool result, not just the answer. Tool calls
      during a task use normal model tokens and may count against the service’s quota.
    </p>
    <p>
      By default pi doesn’t send every tool’s schema with every request. The agent finds tools with
      a search and calls them from a short script, which shows in the chat as “Ran a script”.
      Setting a server’s exposure to direct skips that and uses more context on every request.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="catalog">
    <h2>What each service needs</h2>
    <div class="connector-requirements">
      {
        connectors.map(({ id, name, use, note, docs }) => (
          <article id={id}>
            <>
              <p class="eyebrow">{use}</p>
              <h3>{name}</h3>
              <p>{note}</p>
              <a href={docs}>{name} setup docs ↗</a>
            </>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p>
      The tools you get depend on the server, your account, and the scopes you grant. Supabase and
      Questrade default to read-only; other connectors don’t. Supabase’s read-only endpoint also
      doesn’t shrink the OAuth grant, so the token still has write scopes. Pin it to a project and
      review its permissions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="custom">
    <h2>Add a custom MCP server</h2>
    <p>
      In MCP Connectors, add a custom server with either a URL or a local command and its arguments,
      and pick a scope. A command-based server runs a program on your machine, so review it like any
      package you install.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check it after saving. A running chat loads the change before its next message. A project
      server replaces a global one with the same name, and pi only reads a project’s config once
      you’ve trusted the folder: run <code>pi</code> in a terminal there and choose Trust.
    </p>
    <p>
      Using your Claude subscription? Add servers here too. Phosphor keeps the Claude CLI’s own MCP
      config and claude.ai connectors out on purpose, so they aren’t imported.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Permissions and troubleshooting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Needs sign-in:</strong> use Sign in for the account you want.</li><li>
        <strong>Down:</strong> check the server URL, your network, and anything a local command needs.
        Then Check again.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Slack’s Add is disabled:</strong> finish setting up your Slack app first: client ID, redirect
        URI, PKCE, and scopes. “Set up the app” on the card has the manifest.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Tools missing from a running chat:</strong> send another message. Phosphor reloads a chat’s
        MCP config before its next turn when it changed.
      </li><li>
        <strong>The wrong config wins:</strong> check scope and overrides in Advanced instead of copying
        credentials into more files.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Grant only the access the agent needs. Some tools can change data in the service. Worktrees
      and the artifact sandbox don’t limit what a connected service can do. Keep credentials and
      callback URLs out of bug reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('mcp.md')}>MCP configuration, scope resolution, and OAuth reference</a> · <a
        href={piDoc('mcp')}>pi’s built-in MCP</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
